\subsection{Harmonic vs Anharmonic Contributions}

For the harmonic RS case ($\lambda_4 = 0$), the effective potential is quadratic:
\begin{equation}
V_{\text{harm}}''(\Phi) = 2\lambda.
\end{equation}

In the anharmonic case,
\begin{equation}
V_{\text{anharm}}''(\Phi) = 2\lambda + 12\lambda_4 \Phi^2.
\end{equation}

The quartic term introduces a $y$-dependent shift in the effective potential,
modifying the eigenvalue spectrum.

